        "prompt":  "Make the image from prompt.\n\nPrompt\n\nCreate a professional educational infographic worksheet for Social Studies / Local History class on the topic “Ping River, Chiang Mai” for upper elementary / lower secondary students. The worksheet should be designed in a modern, visually engaging educational infographic style with vibrant colors, clean typography, and highly readable layouts that encourage Historical Thinking, Geographical Thinking, and Environmental Thinking skills.\n\nThe worksheet must emphasize:\n\n* Inquiry-Based Learning\n* Active Learning\n* Critical Thinking\n* Future Thinking\n\nThe learning experience should encourage students to:\n\n* investigate geographical and historical evidence\n* observe and analyze river ecosystems and local communities\n* connect causes and effects between humans and nature\n* compare the past and present condition of the Ping River\n* predict possible future environmental and community changes\n\nThe focus should NOT be rote memorization, but meaningful understanding of local history, geography, environment, and sustainable development.\n\nMAIN TITLE:\n“Educational Worksheet : Ping River, Chiang Mai”\n\nInclude:\n\n* student name section\n* class\n* number\n* date\n\nVISUAL STYLE:\n\n* modern educational infographic worksheet\n* contemporary museum-exhibition aesthetic\n* premium Thai educational media design\n* visually organized section cards\n* clean and colorful educational layout\n* Pixar-inspired educational atmosphere\n* highly engaging but academically professional\n\nCOLOR PALETTE:\nUse nature-inspired Northern Thai colors:\n\n* river blue\n* emerald green\n* warm gold\n* teak brown\n* soft sky blue\n* contemporary Thai pastel accents\n\nGRAPHIC ELEMENTS:\nInclude educational icons and visuals such as:\n\n* maps of Chiang Mai and the Ping River\n* bridges and riverside communities\n* traditional Lanna boats\n* temples along the river\n* fish and river ecosystems\n* magnifying glass\n* timeline graphics\n* comparison charts\n* environmental and cultural symbols\n\nCHARACTER ELEMENT:\nInclude a friendly educational guide character:\n“Krucopter” or a young river explorer guide\nin a cute 3D Pixar-style educational character design.\n\nLAYOUT STRUCTURE:\nVertical A4 printable layout\nClear section hierarchy\nReadable Thai/English educational typography\nHigh-resolution printable quality\nPremium classroom exhibition design\n\nAdd the educational slogan:\n“Learn from the River, Understand the Community, Design a Sustainable Future.”\n\nThe worksheet should contain 7 clearly separated sections with elegant infographic-style cards.\n\nSECTION 1 — LEARNING OBJECTIVES\nStudents should be able to:\n\n* explain the importance of the Ping River to Chiang Mai\n* analyze how rivers influence communities and culture\n* study environmental and historical changes of the river\n* connect river ecosystems with modern urban life\n* predict future environmental impacts and conservation solutions\n\nSECTION 2 — PART A : INQUIRY STARTER\nCreate a local inquiry activity with guiding questions such as:\n\n“Why did ancient communities choose to settle near the Ping River?”\n\n“How would life in Chiang Mai change if the Ping River disappeared?”\n\nInclude:\n\n* river map illustrations\n* observation visuals\n* exploration space\n\nAdd a thinking table:\n“What I Observe”\n“What I Wonder”\n“What I Predict”\n\nEncourage curiosity and evidence-based thinking.\n\nSECTION 3 — PART B : RIVER TIMELINE EXPLORER\nCreate a large infographic timeline including major developments such as:\n\n* early Lanna settlements near the river\n* river transportation and trade\n* agricultural development\n* modern urban expansion along the Ping River\n* environmental conservation projects\n\nActivities:\n\n* fill in missing historical/environmental events\n* arrange events chronologically\n* analyze which development changed the river most and explain why\n\nInclude:\n\n* timeline graphics\n* environmental icons\n* arrows and visual flow\n\nSECTION 4 — PART C : CAUSE \u0026 EFFECT ANALYSIS\nCreate a thinking chart with columns:\n“Event or Change”\n“Cause”\n“Effect”\n“What if this never happened?”\n\nExample topics:\n\n* urban expansion\n* flooding\n* deforestation upstream\n* tourism growth\n* river conservation projects\n\nStrong emphasis on:\nCause-and-Effect Thinking\nEnvironmental reasoning\nCritical analysis\n\nSECTION 5 — PART D : PAST VS PRESENT\nCompare:\n“Ping River in the Past” vs “Ping River Today”\n\nComparison topics:\n\n* transportation\n* water quality\n* riverside communities\n* environment\n* agriculture\n* tourism and economy\n\nUse:\n\n* Venn Diagram\n* comparison chart\n* infographic comparison visuals\n\nEncourage students to identify continuity and change over time.\n\nSECTION 6 — PART E : FUTURE RIVER DESIGNER\nEncourage Future Thinking activities such as:\n\n“If pollution in the Ping River continues, what might Chiang Mai look like in the future?”\n\n“How should communities protect and develop the Ping River sustainably over the next 50 years?”\n\nProvide writing space for:\n\n* creative environmental proposals\n* future city ideas\n* conservation plans\n* sustainable community concepts\n\nSECTION 7 — REFLECTION\nReflection prompts:\n“Today I discovered that…”\n“The most important role of the Ping River is…”\n“A lesson from the Ping River that can help the future is…”\n\nInclude self-assessment checkboxes for:\n\n* investigation skills\n* analytical thinking\n* environmental awareness\n* comparison skills\n* future prediction skills\n* teamwork and participation\n\nIMAGE \u0026 DESIGN DETAILS:\n\n* printable 4K quality\n* vertical A4 composition\n* modern classroom infographic design\n* Pixar-inspired environmental classroom aesthetic\n* realistic educational poster quality\n* premium Thai teaching-media appearance\n* organized but visually exciting\n* balanced use of whitespace and infographic density\n\nIMPORTANT DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:\n\n* prioritize questioning over memorization\n* encourage exploration and environmental reasoning\n* visually beautiful and highly organized\n* stimulate curiosity and active participation\n* feel like a professional teacher-created educational exhibition worksheet",
