        "prompt":  "Create a highly detailed illustrated infographic poster \n\nCHIANG MAI, THAILAND\n\nGOAL:\nShow the city as a giant discoverable world full of tiny hidden places, local secrets, iconic activities, food spots, and unexpected moments.\n\nThis should feel like:\nan illustrated hidden object world + modern infographic + animated city poster. The focus must be:\nBIG visuals, BIG icons, minimal text.\n\n---\n\nMAIN STRUCTURE:\n\nVertical 4:5 poster  \nLarge illustrated city environment  \nDense but very readable composition  \nMinimal text, mostly visual storytelling\n\n---\n\nMAIN CONCEPT:\n\nThe city should feel alive and overflowing with discoveries.\n\nEvery corner should contain:\n- hidden moments\n- mini activities\n- local culture\n- food\n- tiny stories\n- funny details\n\nPeople should zoom into the poster for minutes.\n\n---\n\nCENTERPIECE:\n\nCreate a large stylized illustrated version of Chiang Mai, Thailand.\n\nInclude:\n- Wat Phra That Doi Suthep overlooking the mountains\n- Chiang Mai Old City moat and ancient brick walls\n- Tha Phae Gate with pigeons and tourists\n- Nimmanhaemin cafés and creative spaces\n- Warorot Market street-food atmosphere\n- Sunday Walking Street market\n- Ping River waterfront\n- mountain roads and forest scenery around Doi Inthanon\n- elephant sanctuaries and jungle eco-tourism\n- lantern festivals inspired by Yi Peng\n- traditional Lanna wooden houses\n- tuk-tuks, red songthaews, bicycles, and scooters\n\nThe city should look:\nfun, animated, layered, and explorable.\n\n---\n\nINFOGRAPHIC SYSTEM (VERY IMPORTANT):\n\nUse LARGE visual icons and symbols instead of long text.\n\nExamples:\n- khao soi bowl icon\n- coffee cup icon\n- mountain hiking icon\n- lantern festival icon\n- temple icon\n- elephant icon\n- night market icon\n- hidden café icon\n- handicraft umbrella icon\n- street-food icon\n- art gallery icon\n- waterfall icon\n\nEach icon should:\n- clearly represent an activity or vibe\n- be visually bold and easy to spot\n\n---\n\nTEXT RULES:\n\nUse:\n- tiny labels only\n- short names\n- mini reactions\n- 1–3 words max\n\nExamples:\n“Khao Soi”\n“Hidden Café”\n“Night Vibes”\n“Temple View”\n“Local Crafts”\n“River Walk”\n\nNO:\n- paragraphs\n- information blocks\n- long descriptions\n\n---\n\nVISUAL DETAILS:\n\nAdd MANY tiny hidden scenes:\n\n- monks walking in the early morning\n- musicians playing traditional Lanna instruments\n- cats sleeping near cafés\n- tourists eating khao soi and sai ua\n- artists sketching temples\n- coffee lovers in specialty cafés\n- couples taking photos during Yi Peng lantern festival\n- scooter riders exploring small alleys\n- rooftop dinners with mountain views\n- hidden craft workshops in Bo Sang\n- local flower markets\n- hill tribe textile sellers\n- street vendors grilling food\n- backpackers checking maps\n- baristas preparing latte art\n- hikers at Doi Inthanon trails\n\n---\n\nMAP ELEMENTS:\n\nInclude:\n- walking paths\n- mini routes\n- arrows\n- area markers\n- transportation symbols\n\nAdd:\n- compass\n- legend icons\n- “YOU ARE HERE” marker\n\n---\n\nSTYLE:\n\n- comic + infographic hybrid\n- animated movie environment\n- playful but premium\n- highly detailed\n- organized chaos\n\nInspired by:\n- hidden object games\n- illustrated travel maps\n- Pixar environment art\n- modern infographic posters\n\n---\n\nCOLOR SYSTEM:\n\nUse:\n- warm Lanna-inspired colors\n- tropical greens\n- golden temple tones\n- earthy brick reds\n- lantern oranges\n- river blues\n- café neutrals\n- vibrant market accents\n\nKeep:\n- clear hierarchy\n- easy navigation\n\n---\n\nLAYOUT:\n\nMix:\n- giant city environment\n- oversized infographic icons\n- small hidden scenes\n- map markers\n- mini comic interactions\n\nThe poster must feel:\ndense but clean.\n\n---\n\nDEPTH:\n\n- 50–100 tiny visual elements\n- multiple layers of discoveries\n- rich environmental storytelling\n\n---\n\nIMPORTANT RULES:\n\n- prioritize visuals over text\n- icons must be large and readable\n- avoid cluttered paragraphs\n- no empty spaces\n- every section should contain discoveries\n- city references must feel specific to Chiang Mai, Thailand\n- architecture, food, transportation, festivals, and nature must accurately reflect real Chiang Mai culture and geography\n\n---\n\nFINAL FEEL:\n\nLike:\n- a giant illustrated hidden city adventure\n- a visual discovery infographic\n- a poster people endlessly zoom into\n\nNOT:\n- text-heavy travel guide\n- flat map\n- chaotic unreadable collage",
