        "prompt":  "Here is the adapted Nano Banana prompt, preserving the full structural framework while transforming the content into Thai Classical Music Performance (การแสดงดนตรีไทย):\n\n⸻\n\nHere is another fun Nano Banana prompt for traditional music lovers.\n\n\u003cinstruction\u003e  \n\n\nInput A is a Traditional Thai Classical Music Performance (e.g., ระบำสุโขทัย, เพลงลาวดวงเดือน, โหมโรงเย็น, การแสดงปี่พาทย์ไม้แข็ง).\n\n⸻\n\n1. Inference Engine\n\nInput A is a Traditional Thai Musical Composition or Performance.\nDeconstruct the music into 3 Physical Assets:\n\nThe Waveform (The Mountain):\nIdentify the primary structural sections of the Thai composition (e.g., โหมโรง, ท่อนช้า, ท่อนเร็ว, ลูกหมด, จบเพลง) or traditional tempo progression (ช้า–กลาง–เร็ว).\n\nThe Maestro (The Figure):\nIdentify the musical leader — such as the ครูเพลง, หัวหน้าวงปี่พาทย์, หรือวาทยกรดนตรีไทย — to act as the conductor/focal point.\n\nThe Ensemble (The Scale):\nIdentify the specific Thai traditional instrumentation required (e.g., วงปี่พาทย์ไม้แข็ง, วงเครื่องสายไทย, วงมโหรี), including instruments such as ระนาดเอก, ระนาดทุ้ม, ฆ้องวงใหญ่, ปี่ใน, ฉิ่ง, ตะโพน, ซอด้วง, จะเข้.\n\n⸻\n\n2. Container\n\nGoal: A “Collector’s Edition Thai Classical Audio Sculpture” Diorama.\n\nThe Stage:\nA large, circular, dark-teakwood Traditional Thai Performance Stage/Plinth, with subtle carved Thai motifs around the base (Lanna or Rattanakosin style depending on the piece).\n\nThe Plaque:\nA gold or brass nameplate on the front edge detailing:\n• Composition Title (Thai script)\n• Composer (if known, e.g., traditional royal court composer)\n• Historical Era or First Recorded Performance Date\n\nThe Packaging:\nIn the blurred background, include a high-end “Collector’s Box” featuring Thai ornamental patterns and a Certificate of Authenticity with traditional Thai border designs.\n\n⸻\n\n3. Sculpture (The Solid Sound)\n\nThe Waveform Background:\nA massive, continuous Translucent Resin Sculpture rises at the back of the circular stage. It represents a physical soundwave interpretation of the entire Thai musical composition.\n\nThe Peaks:\nThe peaks and valleys correspond to rhythmic density, melodic ornamentation, and tempo transitions (slow ceremonial passages to energetic rhythmic climaxes).\n\nThe Musical Notation:\nInstead of Western staves, stylized Thai music notation symbols (เลขไทยระบบโน้ตไทย) and rhythmic markings are embedded or etched directly into the translucent “mountain” surface.\n\n⸻\n\n4. Narrative Scale (The Performance)\n\nThe Conductor:\nA stylized, slightly oversized (Chibi/Caricature) figure of the Thai musical leader (ครูดนตรีไทย) stands at the center-front, seated cross-legged or standing traditionally, subtly directing the ensemble.\n\nThe Ensemble:\nDozens of tiny 1:87 scale musician figures are seated traditionally on the stage floor, each playing miniature Thai instruments with accurate posture and hand positioning.\n\nThe Audience:\nTiny figures in traditional Thai attire (historic era-appropriate clothing depending on the composition’s origin) stand around the base of the plinth, respectfully watching or applauding.\n\n⸻\n\n5. Visual\n\nLighting:\nTheatrical rigging inspired by a Thai cultural performance hall. Warm golden stage spotlights shine downward dramatically.\n\nThe Glow:\nThe translucent “Waveform Mountain” is illuminated from within, shifting colors across its width to reflect the emotional progression of the piece (e.g., warm amber → deep crimson → royal gold).\n\nThe Labels:\nClean, elegant Thai and English floating text above the mountain peaks labeling sections (e.g., “โหมโรง”, “ท่อนช้า”, “ท่อนเร็ว”, “จบเพลง”).\n\n⸻\n\nOutput:\nONE image, 1:1 Aspect Ratio, 3D Render, “High-End Cultural Collectible” aesthetic, Dramatic Stage Lighting.",
