        "prompt":  "Create a collage-style visual poster with the atmosphere of a Chinese public notice board, reimagined for Chiang Mai cultural tourism. Let an enormous distressed Chinese-style headline become the dominant visual weight of the composition. The typography should feel like rough woodblock printing or screen printing, as if heavily pressed with ink onto paper. Edges should contain imperfections, missing ink, rough grain, dry-brush textures, registration shifts, and subtle printing defects. The oversized headline should span across the main visual area and immediately command attention.\n\nThe central subject should enter the composition as a real photograph or specimen-like cutout rather than a smooth illustration. Use authentic photographic fragments of Chiang Mai cultural icons, enlarged, cropped, partially obscured, layered, or torn as if attached to the paper surface as documentary evidence. Subjects may include Yi Peng lanterns, Doi Suthep Temple, Bo Sang umbrellas, traditional Lanna architecture, artisans, coffee culture, mountain landscapes, local markets, or Northern Thai cultural objects. The photographic elements should feel collected, archived, and physically pasted onto the poster.\n\nThe background should be built from large areas of light paper stock, torn paper edges, translucent color fields, stamp impressions, tracing marks, handwritten annotations, postal marks, ticket fragments, map textures, archive numbers, dates, locations, bilingual notes, and documentary-style information. Information density should be high yet visually organized: the giant headline dominates the scene, the photographic subjects occupy the middle layer, while smaller text elements appear along edges, corners, narrow columns, circular seals, and information blocks, creating the rhythm of a museum exhibition notice or public cultural announcement.\n\nColors should be extracted naturally from Chiang Mai’s landscape, architecture, festivals, crafts, and seasonal atmosphere rather than relying on fixed palettes. Preserve large areas of bright clean paper tones and spacious color fields. Use a limited number of high-saturation accent colors inspired by lantern light, temple gold, saffron robes, or traditional textiles. Maintain strong dark typography and translucent supporting color layers. The overall result should feel bright, impactful, breathable, and contemporary, with grain appearing only through printing textures and paper surfaces.\n\nAll visual elements should appear scanned, photocopied, overprinted, torn, stamped, layered, and manually rearranged. Preserve visible human intervention through collage edges, registration shifts, overlapping fragments, masking tape traces, paper tears, and archival markings. The poster should combine the authority of historical documentation with the immediacy of contemporary street posters.\n\nAvoid excessive neatness, plastic surfaces, artificial AI glow effects, empty decorative ornaments, glossy finishes, photorealistic lighting effects, or overly polished layouts.\n\nTheme:\nCHIANG MAI\n\nPurpose:\nChiang Mai Cultural Tourism Campaign Poster 2026\n\nSuggested Main Headline:\nCHIANG MAI\nTHE HEART OF LANNA\n\nAspect Ratio:\n9:16 Vertical\n\nVisual Keywords:\nChinese Public Notice Aesthetic, Archival Collage Design, Woodblock Typography, Screen Print Texture, Documentary Layout, Cultural Exhibition Poster, Chiang Mai Heritage, Yi Peng Festival, Lanna Architecture, Museum Archive Graphics, Street Poster Energy, Layered Printmaking, Contemporary Cultural Branding.",
