        "prompt":  "T=“standard” id=“54728”}\nWhat makes this kind of image difficult?\n\nAI is good at being “clean” and “polished.”\n\nBut a traditional Northern Thai street-food poster needs to feel “loud,” “busy,” and full of life.\n\nThe information density is extremely high — dish name, ingredients, spice levels, handwritten notes, stamps, promotional labels, local descriptions, regional references, food close-ups, bowls, garnishes, and cultural details all have to coexist on a single poster without falling apart visually.\n\nThe typography must feel authentic and energetic without becoming messy. The vintage print textures must feel nostalgic without looking damaged. The broth must look rich and steaming hot, not like a plastic model. The chili oil must feel spicy. The pickled mustard greens must look fresh. The pork ribs, tomatoes, and herbs must feel appetizing and real.\n\nA good Khanom Jeen Nam Ngiao poster should feel like something you would discover at a beloved local restaurant in Chiang Mai, not a sterile food advertisement.\n\nIn one sentence:\n\nThe difference between authentic street-food charm and cheap visual clutter is a matter of design judgment.\n\n“Create a visually dense Chiang Mai street-food advertising poster for Khanom Jeen Nam Ngiao, packed with handwritten typography, vintage print elements, menu annotations, ingredient callouts, red stamps, local restaurant atmosphere, steaming noodle bowls, and authentic Northern Thai food culture. Chaotic but perfectly organized.”",
