        "category":  "สื่อการสอน",
        "prompt":  "Create a single refined contemplative editorial poster based on Buddhist philosophy, designed as a high-end modern visual essay.\n\nThe composition should follow a minimalist, high-breathing-space aesthetic with large areas of high-brightness negative space. The background is divided into two very subtle horizontal tonal fields (upper and lower), both extremely soft and calm, with a fine paper grain texture that remains clean, airy, and unobtrusive. The overall feeling should resemble a museum-grade philosophical publication page.\n\nTheme: Buddhist Philosophy — Impermanence (Anicca)\n\nMain Visual Concept:\nA delicate, hand-drawn symbolic object representing impermanence emerges from the lower visual weight of the composition: a partially dissolving sand mandala transitioning into drifting particles of light and dust-like fragments. The form is not centered but slightly offset to create contemplative imbalance. Portions of the mandala subtly extend upward as if dissolving into the atmosphere, visually expressing continuous change and transformation.\n\nSecondary Symbol:\nA minimal floating circular mark positioned in the upper-right or upper-center area, slightly translucent and softly overlapping the background fields. It represents time, cycle, and continuity. The circle is imperfect, gently fading at the edges.\n\nTypography as Structural Design:\n- A large vertical English headline in elegant serif typography:\n  IMPERMANENCE\n- A secondary bold philosophical phrase placed with deliberate spacing:\n  NOTHING REMAINS THE SAME\n- A small refined explanatory line in compact, spaced typography:\n  “All conditioned things are in constant flux; understanding this is the beginning of wisdom.”\n- A minimal index-style footer line aligned at the bottom edge:\n  BUDDHIST PHILOSOPHY / CONTEMPLATIVE SERIES / 01\n\nTypography must act as architectural structure, not decoration—balanced, calm, and highly readable, with generous spacing and controlled hierarchy.\n\nColor System:\nDerived from contemplative Buddhist visual language:\n- soft ivory white (dominant background)\n- muted sand beige (secondary field)\n- faded temple gold (accent of mandala dissolution)\n- ink gray (typography)\n- extremely subtle warm gray highlights\n\nAll colors must remain high-key, soft, and breathable, avoiding heaviness or visual noise.\n\nComposition Rules:\n- The main symbolic form sits in the lower third\n- The floating circle occupies the upper third\n- Typography anchors the vertical rhythm in the center-left or center alignment\n- Strong asymmetry with controlled balance\n- Negative space is a core design element, not empty space\n\nTexture and Materiality:\n- Fine rice paper grain texture\n- Slight ink absorption effect on drawn elements\n- Very subtle pigment diffusion on edges of dissolving forms\n- No harsh outlines or heavy contrast edges\n\nOverall Mood:\nA quiet philosophical meditation rendered as a contemporary editorial art page—calm, intellectual, spiritual, and refined. It should feel like a collectible printed page from a high-end exhibition catalog exploring Buddhist thought.\n\nNegative constraints:\nNo photorealism, no 3D rendering, no clutter, no heavy gradients, no dark mood, no collage, no chaotic typography, no decorative ornaments, no overly saturated colors, no illustration-style cartooning.",
