        "category":  "สื่อการสอน",
        "prompt":  "\nGenerate a vertical 3:4 cinematic editorial poster themed around **\"Chiang Mai PM2.5 Air Pollution.\"**\n\nThe overall concept should **not** resemble a public health campaign, government awareness poster, environmental advertisement, or scientific infographic. Instead, present it as a **conceptual cinematic art poster** exploring the emotional aftermath of pollution—*\"When the mountains disappear / When the city forgets the sky / Silence beneath the haze / Waiting for clean air.\"*\n\nThe atmosphere should communicate loneliness, endurance, uncertainty, memory, resilience, and hope through symbolic environmental objects rather than literal disaster imagery.\n\n---\n\n## Main Subject\n\nThe composition revolves around **one single symbolic object**, for example:\n\n- an abandoned N95 mask partially buried in roadside dust\n- a weathered air-quality monitor covered with ash\n- a broken air purifier standing alone\n- an empty park bench disappearing into haze\n- a dried leaf coated in fine PM2.5 dust\n- a bicycle covered with gray particles\n- a faded school flag beneath thick smog\n- a cracked window looking toward invisible mountains\n- a silent street lamp fading into polluted air\n- a traditional Northern Thai Lanna lantern dimmed by dense haze\n\nThe symbolic object must be entirely original.\n\nAvoid visual similarities to common dystopian references:\n- no giant industrial machinery\n- no engine parts\n- no road-center composition\n- no copied cinematic framing\n- no obvious apocalypse clichés\n\nInstead, create a new visual metaphor representing life beneath persistent PM2.5 pollution.\n\n---\n\n## Environment\n\nLarge empty spaces.\n\nHeavy atmospheric haze.\n\nWet pavement after light rain.\n\nSoft reflections on damp concrete.\n\nInvisible mountain silhouettes hidden behind dense smog.\n\nLow-saturation gray-white sky.\n\nDust-covered sidewalks.\n\nAsh-like airborne particles.\n\nScattered dried leaves.\n\nVery distant city lights barely visible through haze.\n\nSubtle warm remnants of sunlight struggling through pollution.\n\nThe environment should feel cinematic, quiet, melancholic, restrained, and emotionally powerful.\n\n---\n\n## Visual Style\n\nDominant palette:\n\n- charcoal gray\n- soft concrete white\n- muted ash gray\n- cool fog gray\n\nAccent colors:\n\n- muted amber\n- warning orange\n- subdued deep red\n\nUse accent colors sparingly to guide visual attention.\n\nAvoid vivid colors.\n\nAvoid commercial environmental campaign aesthetics.\n\nAvoid cheerful or optimistic imagery.\n\nMaintain a premium fine-art poster quality.\n\n---\n\n## Typography\n\nPlace the main English title in the upper-left corner or vertically along the left edge:\n\n# CHIANG MAI PM2.5\n\nSubtitle:\n\n**【Health】**\n\nShort poetic statement:\n\n**【Breath】**\n\nTypography should be elegant, contemporary, and highly legible, inspired by modern film posters or museum exhibition graphics.\n\nEnglish should be the primary language. Small supporting text may be included but should remain subtle.\n\n---\n\n## Composition\n\n1. Large areas of negative space.\n2. Main subject positioned in the lower third or lower-right quadrant.\n3. The symbolic object should cast a subtle reflection on the wet ground.\n4. In the far background, only faint silhouettes of Chiang Mai\u0027s mountains, scattered trees, distant temples, or barely visible city lights emerging through the haze.\n5. Typography should resemble a premium contemporary art exhibition poster or an award-winning cinematic movie poster.\n6. The emotional focus should be **after the pollution has settled**, emphasizing silence, memory, and reflection rather than an active environmental disaster.\n\n---\n\n## Texture Keywords\n\nPM2.5 dust\n\nair pollution\n\nmorning haze\n\nmist\n\nwet asphalt\n\nfine ash\n\nweathered concrete\n\nsoft reflections\n\ncracks\n\nfaded surfaces\n\nresidual light\n\nsilence\n\nwaiting\n\nmemory\n\nbreath\n\ninvisible mountains\n\nNorthern Thailand\n\nChiang Mai\n\n---\n\n## Negative Prompt\n\nNo watermarks.\n\nNo government logos.\n\nNo environmental organization logos.\n\nNo QR codes.\n\nNo commercial advertising.\n\nNo public awareness campaign style.\n\nNo photobashed collage.\n\nNo visible crowds.\n\nNo recognizable individuals.\n\nNo oversized respirators dominating the composition.\n\nNo industrial factory focus.\n\nNo oversized vehicles.\n\nNo sensational disaster imagery.\n\nNo excessive visual clutter.\n\nNo bright saturated colors.\n\nNo optimistic environmental graphics.\n\nThe final artwork should feel like a collectible contemporary editorial poster—a quiet visual meditation on Chiang Mai\u0027s annual PM2.5 season, where absence, silence, and memory become the true subjects.",
