        "prompt":  "\n\nGenerate a finished vertical **3:4 museum exhibition poster** themed around **\"Celadon Legacy: The Art of Sukhothai Sangkhalok Ceramics.\"**\n\nThe overall direction should embody:\n**Sukhothai ceramic aesthetics, museum exhibition design, elegant English typography, fine artifact photography, decorative ceramic motifs, Southeast Asian heritage, contemporary editorial layout, and refined cultural storytelling.**\n\n---\n\n## Main Subject\n\nThe primary focus is **one Sukhothai Sangkhalok ceramic artifact**, for example:\n\n- a classic Sukhothai celadon bowl\n- carved celadon bowl with lotus motifs\n- fish-pattern Sangkhalok bowl\n- celadon covered jar\n- Sukhothai ceramic vase\n- celadon plate\n- ceramic shard from an archaeological excavation\n- enlarged ceramic decorative patterns\n- museum archive catalog presentation of Sangkhalok ceramics\n\nThe ceramic object must beautifully express:\n\n- elegant vessel proportions\n- lustrous celadon glaze\n- refined carved or painted motifs\n- historical authenticity\n- handcrafted kiln-fired texture\n\nSurface details should include subtle glaze reflections, delicate crackle, carved lotus patterns, fish motifs, floral decorations, soft celadon green, warm ivory ceramic body, aged bronze accents, charcoal gray detailing, and naturally muted heritage colors.\n\nThe artwork should **never resemble a commercial product advertisement or e-commerce listing**, but instead feel like a prestigious international museum exhibition poster.\n\n---\n\n## Choose One Visual Direction\n\nThe artwork may follow **one** of these exhibition aesthetics:\n\n1. Warm ivory background + celadon bowl + minimalist editorial typography\n2. Deep charcoal background + gold typography + dramatic museum spotlight\n3. Traditional Sukhothai earthy terracotta background + elegant gold borders\n4. Modern white gallery background + oversized exhibition title + archive layout\n5. Handmade mulberry paper texture + celadon ceramics + minimal landscape composition\n6. Archaeological archive paper + annotation lines + enlarged ceramic details\n7. Artistic collage of ceramic fragments + reconstructed Sangkhalok patterns\n8. Deep blue spotlight environment + close-up hero ceramic artifact + luxury museum display\n9. Soft jade-green decorative background with lotus clouds and traditional Thai ornamental motifs\n\n---\n\n## Typography\n\nMain Exhibition Title:\n\n**CELADON LEGACY**\n\nSubtitle:\n\n**The Art of Sukhothai Sangkhalok Ceramics**\n\nExhibition Date:\n\n**2026.07.03**\n\nVenue:\n\n**Sukhothai Heritage Museum**\n\nOpening Hours:\n\n**Tuesday–Sunday | 09:30–17:30**\n\nOrganized by:\n\n**Sukhothai Heritage Museum**\n\nAcademic Support:\n\n**Institute of Southeast Asian Ceramic Studies**\n\nTypography must be perfectly legible, professionally typeset, and free from errors.\n\nEnglish should remain the dominant language.\n\nSmall supporting text is acceptable, for example:\n\n*\"Echoes of Celadon\"*\n\n*\"Masterpieces from the Kilns of Ancient Sukhothai\"*\n\nThe supporting English text should remain secondary and never compete with the main title.\n\n---\n\n## Information Hierarchy\n\nThe poster should include a complete exhibition information structure:\n\n- Main exhibition title\n- Subtitle\n- Short exhibition introduction\n- Exhibition date\n- Venue\n- Opening hours\n- Organizing institution\n- Academic support\n- Decorative ceramic motifs and museum-style graphic elements\n\n---\n\n## Composition\n\n1. The Sukhothai Sangkhalok ceramic artifact must be the undisputed visual centerpiece.\n\n2. Typography should establish a clear visual relationship with the artifact rather than appearing randomly placed.\n\n3. The design may incorporate:\n\n- elegant vertical typography\n- horizontal editorial headings\n- museum catalog labels\n- archival annotation lines\n- collector\u0027s seals\n- fine decorative borders\n- enlarged ceramic ornament details\n- exhibition information cards\n\n4. The overall design should feel:\n\n- refined\n- timeless\n- restrained\n- museum quality\n- sophisticated\n- rooted in Southeast Asian heritage\n\nAvoid trendy pop aesthetics or overly decorative \"heritage tourism\" styles.\n\nThe finished artwork should be polished enough for immediate publication as an international museum exhibition poster.\n\n---\n\n## Color Palette\n\nUse authentic Sukhothai ceramic-inspired colors:\n\n- celadon green\n- soft jade\n- warm ivory\n- kiln-fired beige\n- weathered clay\n- charcoal gray\n- aged bronze\n- muted gold\n- deep forest green accents\n\nMaintain elegant low-saturation harmony throughout.\n\n---\n\n## Negative Prompt\n\nNo watermarks.\n\nNo commercial brand logos.\n\nNo QR codes.\n\nNo promotional discount graphics.\n\nNo e-commerce appearance.\n\nNo multiple dates.\n\nNo outdated years.\n\nNo cluttered layout.\n\nNo cheap souvenir aesthetics.\n\nNo exaggerated fantasy styling.\n\nNo photobashed collage.\n\nNo artificial AI text artifacts.\n\nNo incorrect historical motifs.\n\nNo modern product packaging.\n\nNo visual elements that distract from the ceramic artifact.\n\nThe final artwork should resemble a premium museum exhibition poster celebrating the timeless craftsmanship and cultural legacy of **Sukhothai Sangkhalok Celadon Ceramics**.",
